1. `option_bag_junk_drawer` — `describeCheckout`

Although `CheckoutOptions` is named, it combines six fields spanning plan selection, actor/location, and execution state. An agent adding another checkout concern may simply append another property, making the bag an ever-growing implicit dependency list.

Refactor into narrower records and destructure the owned fields:

```ts
interface CheckoutContext {
  plan: string;
  role: string;
  region: string;
  currency: string;
}

interface CheckoutState {
  status: string;
  shouldRetry: boolean;
}

export function describeCheckout(
  context: CheckoutContext,
  state: CheckoutState,
): string {
  const { plan, role, region, currency } = context;
  const { status, shouldRetry } = state;

  return [
    plan,
    role,
    region,
    currency,
    status,
    String(shouldRetry),
  ].join(":");
}
```

This ties each future field to a specific concept. Today, an agent could add a property that is required only in one mode, make it globally optional, or rename a field without recognizing all six are part of the serialized output.

2. `return_shape_roulette` — `resolvePaymentResult`

The function returns three shapes with as little as 0% key overlap and no declared return type. Introduce an explicit discriminated union:

```ts
type PaymentResult =
  | { kind: "error"; code?: string; message?: string }
  | { kind: "redirect"; url?: string; permanent: false }
  | { kind: "success"; value?: string; cached: false };

export function resolvePaymentResult(input: PaymentInput): PaymentResult {
  if (input.error) {
    return { kind: "error", code: input.code, message: input.message };
  }

  if (input.redirect) {
    return { kind: "redirect", url: input.url, permanent: false };
  }

  return { kind: "success", value: input.value, cached: false };
}
```

Callers can now narrow on `kind`, and exhaustive switches reveal newly added variants. Today, an agent might assume every result has `ok`, treat the redirect result as success, or add a fourth shape that callers silently ignore. A follow-up improvement would make `PaymentInput` discriminated too, eliminating ambiguous inputs where both `error` and `redirect` are true.

3. `negative_flag_maze` — `canRunBilling`

Replace the two negative flags with positive concepts and return the predicate directly:

```ts
export function canRunBilling(
  isBillingEnabled: boolean,
  isRetryAllowed: boolean,
): boolean {
  return isBillingEnabled && isRetryAllowed;
}
```

Existing callers must negate their old values at the migration boundary:

```ts
canRunBilling(!disableBilling, !skipRetry);
```

Then rename the upstream state so those negations disappear as well. Today, an agent extending `!disableBilling && !skipRetry` could invert only one flag, confuse “skip retry” with “retry failed,” or attach another negated condition with the wrong operator. Positive names also resolve the related `boolean_naming_drift` finding.
